at that are now  empty which are typically the engine bay  in the trunk  we send those designs out to the laser  cutters the water jetters the machine  shops who will then  send us the results and we'll be able to  bring them back into the shop and test  fit them on the car  very quickly we arrive at a final design  and a final set of components that we  can  weld  finish  send off for powder coat and paint and  then it will come back to us for  installation  so in 2021 in the u.s at auction that's  online and in-person auctions 36 000  classic cars were bought in the u.s and  that's a huge increase  even over you know pretty high  pre-pandemic levels so it's about 56  increase over 2019 and that represents  2.2 billion in transactions and that's  an even larger increase so these cars  are going for even more money  that's just auctions the peer-to-peer  market which  people selling their cars to each other  on craigslist facebook and any number of  platforms like that is about 10 times  bigger so it's it's really a vital and  liquid market and it's booming at the  moment  electrifying classic cars  it is a controversial thing i mean  the people that that do this are told  all the time that they are taking the  soul out of a car and that  the sound and the smell is a huge part  of the experience and  there may be some truth to that for some  people  but if they're not getting out to drive  the car  because they're not sure it'll start  that's valid on the other side of the  argument too  it's an act of preservation  it started about a decade ago just with  people who decided to electrify their  cherished classic and some of those  hobby projects turned into businesses  it's a really  interesting skunk works required to kind  of make one of these shops work and  that's one of the reasons why there's  not a ton of them  [Music]  i like to describe myself as a wayward  engineer i  went to engineering school and went to  work in the software industry and uh  spent a long time in the technology  industry  the whole way though  i did what we're told to do which is do  the thing that you're good at to make  the money  so that you can do the thing that you  love  on the weekends on the side and i did  that and i followed the money  and i finally just  couldn't take it anymore  i was sick of hot rodding my cars in my  garage in a couple hours in the evening  after my kids went to bed  i really i wanted to do something that i  loved something i could be passionate  about  i think the opportunity of electrifying  classic cars kind of presented itself as  a unique combination of  the things that i was good at and the  things that i  loved so i could apply technology to  something it was not just a piece of  software for a customer  so that opportunity was something that i  i took i made the leap and i started the  company and here we are  once we've designed and test fit all of  these brackets and components then the  process is really taking these finished  parts and installing everything in the  car  if you look at an electric motor it's  typically a fraction of the size of a  combustion engine and so  oftentimes we can fit the  motor in a single speed reduction  gearbox in the same space where the  original transmission was and the entire  engine bay becomes available for us to  put batteries and other components in it  we're also looking to make sure that  we're not putting stresses on the  chassis in areas that it wasn't designed  to take  if you just put the electric motor in  the engine bay and you put batteries in  the trunk you're going to end up an  incredibly unbalanced and possibly  dangerous car  so we try to distribute the weight of  the batteries  throughout the chassis our goal is to  retain the performance and the  feel of this car  these cars were designed  50 years ago very precisely by hundreds  if not thousands of engineers who are  trying to create a certain drive we want  to respect that as much as possible  then we are wiring everything together  wiring up a car is kind of in two phases  there's the high voltage wiring that  powers the motor and connects the  battery pack to the system  and that is relatively simple and  straightforward but there's another set  of wiring that happens in these cars  that we call vehicle control wiring  we want this car to be driven and  enjoyed the same way that it's always  been with the beautiful gauges that were  designed in the 60s with the  analog sweeping  needles and things like that  nobody's buying this  wanting a tesla right they  don't want to get into a classic car and  have a giant ipad on their  dashboard  there's so many components that we  installed so many different things that  have to come together and it's kind of  like we put an orchestra together for  the first time and  that those first drives when you get in  the car  [Music]  tap your little  conductor's wand on the thing and say  okay let's do this let's try this piece  from the start  [Music]  you go out on a drive and  you know what the drummer is you know  pushing pushing a little too hard and  the  uh you know the trumpets are just just  not quite hitting that high note you  hear all those things you take it all in  and you think okay there's something  here we're going to be able to get there  but we got to work on these things  we are out there putting hundreds if not  thousands of miles on the car making  sure that it responds the way that it  should intuitively drives the way you  would expect it to we're able to  manipulate torque curves and all sorts  of inputs and controls we can change the  way that the motor reacts to the  throttle position if you will we take  this time to tune  each section of this orchestra and bring  it all together so that when you get in  and you drive it it is a  flawless performance one that makes  people  smile  and cry  and engages them in a way that they have  never  driven before and that's the goal  we often get the question 